<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Iwo Jima</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-war">Pacific War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Iwo Jima was fought from 19 February to 26 March 1945, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Marines captured the volcanic island of Iwo Jima from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-war">Pacific War</a>. The Japanese garrison of about 21,000 under General Kuribayashi Tadamichi fought from tunnels and almost all died; American casualties numbered some 26,000, including nearly 7,000 killed. The flag-raising on Mount Suribachi became a famous photograph.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-iwo-jima.jpg" alt="Battle of Iwo Jima" width="320" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joe Rosenthal · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raising_the_Flag_on_Iwo_Jima,_larger_-_edit1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the American capture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marshall-islands">Marshall Islands</a> in early 1944, Japanese commanders expected a drive toward the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariana-islands">Marianas</a> and set up an inner line of defences running north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> through the Volcano Islands. The American invasion, called Operation Detachment, aimed to capture Iwo Jima and its two airfields. In June 1944 Lieutenant General Tadamichi Kuribayashi took command of the defence. He knew his garrison could not win, but hoped to inflict such casualties that the Americans would reconsider invading Japan.</p><p>The Japanese positions were heavily fortified, with bunkers, hidden artillery and 18 kilometres of tunnels. Major General Harry Schmidt, commanding the Marine landing force, asked for a ten-day bombardment, but Rear Admiral William H. P. Blandy insisted on three days, a decision the Marines resented.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The landings began on 19 February 1945. After about an hour, once men and equipment crowded the beach, the Japanese opened fire with machine guns, mortars and heavy artillery. Loose volcanic ash stopped the amphibious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tractor">tractors</a>, and the Marines had to go forward on foot. The 28th Marines crossed the island at its narrowest, about 800 metres, and isolated Mount Suribachi. Kuribayashi had forbidden banzai charges as a waste of resources, and the Japanese fought from their fortifications.</p><p>On 23 February six Marines raised a flag on Suribachi, in the second flag-raising there that day, and Joe Rosenthal's photograph of it won a Pulitzer Prize. The north was harder: rocky terrain favoured the defenders, and the Motoyama Plateau became what the Marines called the meatgrinder. Flamethrowers were used to clear pillboxes and caves.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Of some 21,000 Japanese on the island, only 216 were taken prisoner, and 3,000 or more kept fighting in the caves until they were hunted down. The 36-day assault cost more than 26,000 American casualties, including 6,800 dead. It was the only Marine battle in which American casualties exceeded Japanese. Two Japanese soldiers held out until January 1949. The value of the island remains disputed: William V. Pratt, a retired Chief of Naval Operations, called it useless to the Army and the Navy, though its airfield served escort fighters and emergency landings of B-29s. Lessons from it guided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-okinawa">Battle of Okinawa</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Iwo Jima” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Midway and Guadalcanal, Imperial General Headquarters shifted to a defensive perimeter that contracted steadily as American forces advanced island by island from 1943 to 1945, through the Marianas, the Philippines, <b>Iwo Jima</b>, and Okinawa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
